    "q-96381": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96381",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "At a call center, the time taken for phone calls to be answered varies continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $3$ calls every minute. What is the probability of taking more than $1$ minute to answer the next call? Round your answer to $4$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Since the time until the next call is answered occurs continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next call is answered using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time, in minutes, between calls. Then, since there are $3$ calls per minute on average, the rate is $\\lambda =3$ calls per minute.\n\nThe amount of time before the next call can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =3,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(3).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next call will occur after $1$ minute or more is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 1) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-3} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-3} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3} \\\\  & ≈0.0498.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
