    "q-132546": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132546",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Vehicles arrive at a crossing continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $10$ seconds. Determine the probability that the next vehicle will arrive at the crossing within the next $3$ seconds. Round your answer to four decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Since the time intervals between the arrival of two successive vehicles at the crossing are continuous and independent, and arrivals occur at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next vehicle arrives as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time between the arrival of two successive vehicles at the crossing. Then, since vehicles arrive every $10$ seconds on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/10$ vehicles per second.\n\nThe time between arrivals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =1/10,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next vehicle will arrive at the crossing within the next $3$ seconds is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/10}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3/10}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-3/10} \\\\  & ≈0.2592\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
